Vita: --vita-fast-gx-wake=off|on|verify (platform/vita/runtime/fast_painter.hpp). With the GX worker
(0007) the game thread publishes after every draw (GXEnd, display list call) and wakes the worker,
and the worker wakes a waiting game thread after every processed range, through std::atomic
wait/notify: on this pthread layer each notify bumps a counter and locks a shared waiter-pool mutex
(and broadcasts a condition when a thread waits). On (and verify), the same wakes happen at the
same points with a flag per sleeper and a semaphore: the waker changes what the sleeper waits for
(the published position or the stop flag, the processed position), then reads the sleeper's flag
and takes it to signal only if the sleeper raised it; a sleeper raises its flag, re-reads what it
waits for and only then blocks (a seq_cst fence between the write and the read on both sides, so
one of them sees the other). The FIFO bytes, their order, every publish point, drain() and the
frame overlap's waits are unchanged; only the mechanism that wakes a sleeping thread differs.
verify times every sleep and counts a sleeper still holding its flag two timeouts after what it
waits for changed (a lost wakeup, recovered by the timeout). Off (the default for now,
fast_painter.hpp) keeps std::atomic wait/notify.

